WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.874
Stage fifteen: building your block.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.814
Six periodisation templates, by distance, ship with the engine.

3
00:00:09.914 --> 00:00:16.284
Five phases, and a peak derived from the volume you already hold, never invented.

4
00:00:17.134 --> 00:00:25.304
Camille aims for a forty-two kilometre race on December twenty-seventh. The block skeleton is first computed as a dry run.

5
00:00:25.404 --> 00:00:31.774
Template, date, held volume, availability: twelve weeks reviewed by the guardrails.

6
00:00:32.624 --> 00:00:37.434
And the Trail des Crêtes, seven weeks away? The template needs twelve.

7
00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:41.552
The coach says so, and never squeezes below the minimum.

8
00:00:42.402 --> 00:00:47.092
After an explicit yes, one file per week, never overwriting.

9
00:00:47.192 --> 00:00:52.146
Race week: the day before free, then three days without running.

10
00:00:52.996 --> 00:01:00.014
On the dashboard, the block timeline: one column per week, the tint is the phase, the height the volume.

11
00:01:00.114 --> 00:01:05.092
Each week opens up: slots to fill in, never invented sessions.

12
00:01:05.942 --> 00:01:14.064
Strength follows the phase: strength, strength endurance, eccentric work, then maintenance in the taper.

13
00:01:14.164 --> 00:01:19.118
The session goes to the watch on an explicit yes, never automatically.

14
00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:29.314
A declared pain never opens an exercise by chance. Mild and unknown: we observe. Sharp or too high: we consult.

15
00:01:29.414 --> 00:01:37.920
Only a mild, confirmed, stable niggle opens a gentle routine. Not medical advice, never a diagnosis.

16
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:45.044
Two documentation pages: templates and the skeleton, then strength and prevention.

17
00:01:47.594 --> 00:01:52.740
To go further: the “Periodisation templates” page of the documentation.
